latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the input grid:\n   a. Identify all distinct non-black (non-zero) colored regions using the find_connected_regions method.\n   b. For each region, determine its bounding box (min and max x, y coordinates).\n\n2. Determine the order of colors from outside to inside:\n   a. Calculate the distance of each region's bounding box from the edge of the grid.\n   b. Sort the regions based on this distance, from smallest (outermost) to largest (innermost).\n   c. If two regions have the same distance, use their position (top-left corner) as a tiebreaker.\n\n3. Determine the size of the output grid:\n   a. Count the number of distinct colors (n) in the sorted list from step 2.\n   b. The output grid size will be (2n-1) x (2n-1).\n\n4. Create the output grid:\n   a. Initialize a new ColoredGrid with the calculated size, filled with zeros (black).\n   b. Start with the full grid size for the outermost color.\n   c. For each color in the sorted list:\n      - Fill the current frame with the color.\n      - Move inward by reducing the frame size by 2 in both dimensions.\n   d. The innermost color will be a single cell in the center.\n\n5. Return the resulting ColoredGrid object.\n\nDetailed implementation steps:\n\n1. Analyze the input grid:\n   ```python\n   def analyze_grid(grid):\n       regions = []\n       for color in range(1, 10):  # Assuming colors are 1-9\n           color_regions = grid.find_connected_regions(color)\n           for region in color_regions:\n               min_x = min(x for x, y in region)\n               max_x = max(x for x, y in region)\n               min_y = min(y for x, y in region)\n               max_y = max(y for x, y in region)\n               regions.append({\n                   'color': color,\n                   'bounding_box': (min_x, min_y, max_x, max_y)\n               })\n       return regions\n   ```\n\n2. Determine the order of colors:\n   ```python\n   def order_colors(regions, grid_size):\n       def distance_from_edge(bbox):\n           return min(bbox[0], bbox[1], grid_size - bbox[2] - 1, grid_size - bbox[3] - 1)\n       \n       sorted_regions = sorted(regions, key=lambda r: (distance_from_edge(r['bounding_box']), r['bounding_box'][:2]))\n       return [r['color'] for r in sorted_regions]\n   ```\n\n3. Determine output grid size:\n   ```python\n   def get_output_size(ordered_colors):\n       return 2 * len(ordered_colors) - 1\n   ```\n\n4. Create the output grid:\n   ```python\n   def create_output_grid(ordered_colors):\n       size = get_output_size(ordered_colors)\n       output = ColoredGrid(values=[[0 for _ in range(size)] for _ in range(size)])\n       \n       for i, color in enumerate(ordered_colors):\n           frame_size = size - 2 * i\n           for x in range(frame_size):\n               for y in range(frame_size):\n                   output.values[i + y][i + x] = color\n       \n       return output\n   ```\n\n5. Main solving function:\n   ```python\n   def solve_c658a4bd(input_grid: ColoredGrid) -> ColoredGrid:\n       regions = analyze_grid(input_grid)\n       ordered_colors = order_colors(regions, len(input_grid.values))\n       return create_output_grid(ordered_colors)\n   ```\n\nThis solution follows the observed patterns and should work for all cases, as it focuses on the relative positions of the shapes in the input grid rather than their sizes or specific configurations. It creates a square output grid with odd dimensions and arranges the colors from the outside in based on their positions in the input grid."
